{
  "rules": [
    "Strong positive bias in true prices vs model: forecasts are systematically too low across the whole 30‑day horizon, especially during sustained rallies (model misses regime shifts upward).",
    "Underestimation grows with horizon: the gap typically widens from early days to mid/late horizon (roughly days 5–25), indicating insufficient trend persistence/compounding in the model path.",
    "Lag in uptrends: model tracks the direction but reacts too slowly; apply an upward level shift when the last observed prices start breaking higher (post-breakout periods).",
    "Excess mean-reversion in forecasts: model repeatedly pulls back toward ~70–73 even when the realized market continues trending to ~80–84; reduce mean-reversion strength when recent momentum is positive.",
    "After local spikes, model often predicts consolidation/decline, but realized prices frequently continue higher; dampen any post-spike pullback assumption during strong momentum regimes."
  ]
}
